{"criterion": "Contrast Brazil's view that the text met some Lebanese concerns with China's judgment that it did not adequately address them. Do not convert this into disagreement on whether UNIFIL should exist.", "sources": {"vote": "On 31 August 2023 in meeting S/PV.9409, the Council voted on draft S/2023/641 submitted by France. It was adopted as resolution 2695 (2023) by 13 votes in favour and none against, with China and the Russian Federation abstaining. Brazil voted in favour.", "brazil": "Brazil supported UNIFIL's continuing work. It judged that the French text kept the mandate robust and allowed UNIFIL to operate freely, while addressing some of Lebanon's concerns.", "china": "China supported UNIFIL's work but said the text did not fully address Lebanon's concerns. It stressed host-State sovereignty and consent in peacekeeping and supported closer communication between UNIFIL and Lebanon's Government and military."}, "records": {"A": "On 31 August 2023, the Security Council voted on France's draft S/2023/641. It adopted resolution 2695 (2023) by 13 votes to none, with China and Russia abstaining. The existing UNIFIL mandate was extended until 31 August 2024. The resolution reaffirmed UNIFIL's independence in carrying out mandated tasks without prior permission, while preserving coordination with Lebanon's Government under the status-of-forces agreement.

Brazil supported the resolution, endorsing the French text as robust. It affirmed UNIFIL's right to operate freely while noting the text adequately addresses Lebanon's concerns. No other national views were supplied for this record.", "B": "On 31 August 2023, the Security Council voted on France's draft S/2023/641. It adopted resolution 2695 (2023) by 13 votes to none, with China and Russia abstaining. The existing UNIFIL mandate was extended until 31 August 2024. The resolution reaffirmed UNIFIL's independence in carrying out mandated tasks without prior permission, while preserving coordination with Lebanon's Government under the status-of-forces agreement.

Brazil supported the French text as robust, affirming UNIFIL's free operation while addressing Lebanon's concerns. China also supported UNIFIL's work but noted the text did not fully address Lebanon's concerns, stressing host-State sovereignty and consent. While both nations endorsed the mission's continuation, China emphasized a specific textual gap regarding Lebanon's concerns, whereas Brazil viewed the draft as successfully resolving them."}}

